From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 14:40:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] halium: size userdata from sysfs, not /proc/partitions

resize_userdata_if_needed() read the partition size out of
/proc/partitions under

    case $path in
    /dev/mmcblk*) ... ;;
    /dev/disk*)   ... ;;
    esac

but mountroot sets path=$(readlink -f $part), which on a UFS device
resolves to /dev/sd* - every recent Pixel, bluejay and panther included.
Neither case matches, pblocks stays empty, the "is the partition much
bigger than the filesystem" test is never true, and the filesystem is
left at whatever size it was flashed with. Silently: there is not even a
message.

That was invisible as long as installing meant "fastboot format:ext4
userdata" and pushing files onto it, because the format sizes the
filesystem to the partition. It is fatal to a flashed userdata image,
where a deliberately small ext4 (3.4 GB) is supposed to grow to the real
partition (~100 GB) on first boot - which is how the athena, bluejay and
panther kits install.

sysfs knows the size of every block device, partitions included, in
512-byte sectors and under whatever name it has, so ask it first and keep
the old greps as a fallback. Warn if neither yields a size rather than
carrying on with an empty number.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/scripts/halium
+++ b/scripts/halium
@@ -242,15 +242,29 @@
 
 	path=$1
 
-	# Partition size in 1k blocks
-	case $path in
-	/dev/mmcblk*)
-		pblocks=$(grep ${path#/dev/*} /proc/partitions | awk {'print $3'})
-		;;
-	/dev/disk*)
-		pblocks=$(grep $(basename $(readlink $path)) /proc/partitions | awk {'print $3'})
-		;;
-	esac
+	# Partition size in 1k blocks.
+	# sysfs knows the size of every block device in 512-byte sectors, whatever
+	# it is called. The old /proc/partitions greps only matched /dev/mmcblk*
+	# and /dev/disk*, so on a UFS device (Pixel: userdata resolves to /dev/sd*)
+	# pblocks stayed empty and the filesystem was silently never grown - which
+	# matters now that we ship a small userdata image and rely on this.
+	pblocks=$(cat "/sys/class/block/$(basename $path)/size" 2>/dev/null)
+	if [ -n "$pblocks" ]; then
+		pblocks=$((pblocks / 2))
+	else
+		case $path in
+		/dev/mmcblk*)
+			pblocks=$(grep ${path#/dev/*} /proc/partitions | awk {'print $3'})
+			;;
+		/dev/disk*)
+			pblocks=$(grep $(basename $(readlink $path)) /proc/partitions | awk {'print $3'})
+			;;
+		esac
+	fi
+	if [ -z "$pblocks" ]; then
+		tell_kmsg "WARNING: cannot determine the size of $path, not resizing"
+		return 0
+	fi
 	# Filesystem size in 4k blocks
 	fsblocks=$(dumpe2fs -h $path | grep "Block count" | awk {'print $3'})
 	# Difference between the reported sizes in 1k blocks
-- 
